==Trivia==
*Prior to [[Version 9.1 Update|Version 9.1]], this stage lacked a [[No Continues]] restriction.
*"[[wikipedia:Pure Land|Pure Land]]" is a concept in Mayahayan Buddhism, its many traditions and teachings falling into the branch of Amidism/Pure Land Buddhism. Reaching the Pure Land is the final goal of Amidism and is tantamount to attaining enlightenment.
**The opposite of the Pure Land is the land of the living, also called the Impure Land, which appears in [[Heaven and Hell (Merciless)|Heaven and Hell]]'s Japanese name.
**Courts of Torment's Japanese name, Narakumon (奈落門), translates to "Naraka Gate". A [[wikipedia:Naraka (Buddhism)|Naraka]] is the Buddhist equivalent of Hell.
**Interestingly enough, [[Hannya]]'s Japanese description mentions that she consumes the souls of those who cannot gain enlightenment, with the term used, ''jōbutsu'' (成仏) specifically referring to attaining Buddhahood.
*The Pure Land's Japanese name, Gokuraku Jōdo (獄楽浄土), makes a pun: normally it would translate to "Paradise Pure Land", but it spells ''gokuraku'' (極楽, paradise) with the kanji 獄, meaning "prison". This character is also used in the Japanese word for Hell, ''jigoku'' (地獄).
**Heaven and Hell's Japanese name makes the same pun, but also replaces the word for the Pure Land with that for the Impure Land.

